  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows quite nicely the midlevel low \ncurrently moving over Manitoba, with deep 994mb low pressure \nanalyzed at the surface and a trailing occluded front draped over \nthe Dakotas. RAP analysis places the UP beneath a strong 40-60kt \nsoutherly LLJ, and resulting isentropic ascent has been able to kick \noff a batch of widespread showers (with some embedded thunder). \nShowers have moved out of the western half of the UP, and are \ncurrently moving through the eastern UP. MRMS shows a widespread \n0.10-0.25in over the past six hours over the western UP, with some \nhigher embedded amounts around 0.50-0.75. This is corroborated by \navailable surface observations, and similar amounts are expected \nover the eastern UP before showers largely wrap up around 12Z. \n\nApart from the rain, gusty winds are the other main concern of the \nshort term forecast period. So far tonight, under southerly flow, we \naren't quite tapping into the strongest winds aloft. Still, some 20-\n30mph gusts have been realized tonight, particularly over the \neastern UP and in the downsloping areas over the higher terrain and \ndownsloping areas over the north-central UP. Breezy conditions \ncontinue the rest of the night. As we head into the daytime hours, \nthe surface low moves towards Hudson Bay and the attendant frontal \nboundary will press through the region during the late morning to \nearly afternoon hours, veering winds to the west. Strong pressure \nrises upwards of 1-2 mb/hour and modest upper level cold air \nadvection will work to increase wind gusts on Lake Superior and in \nthe Keweenaw between 40-50 mph with occasional gusts to 55 mph \nshould mixing be able to tap into higher gusts aloft. Elsewhere \nthroughout the UP, expect wind gusts of 20-35mph to be more common. \nA Wind Advisory continues for N Houghton and Keweenaw Counties from \n15-00z Tuesday (10a-8p local). \n\nMoving into the rest of the week, expect generally quieter weather \nas zonal flow in the upper levels and shortwave ridges moving \nthrough the U.P. generally keep active weather away. While this \nshould keep temperatures fairly seasonable for the rest of the week, \nthis does also support dry weather through at least midweek. \nWednesday night, latest guidance brings some weak shortwave action \nto the area, with some low-end rain chances. As the ridge begins to \nshift eastward towards the weekend and another system moves out of \nthe Plains, better rain chances finally work in by Friday night, and \nthis could be accompanied by another period of elevated wind speeds. \nThat said, there is some disagreement on the precise track and \ntiming of this next system.",
